12.4.m:
One who desires to thrive should pour out three puroḍāśa cakes.
Three are these worlds;
having obtained these very worlds, he secures vigor and virility.
Each successive [cake] is larger;
for each successive of these worlds is larger, they are one above the other;
for these worlds are just like this.
There are twelve potsherds,
for they are three four-potsherd [cakes],
for success.
He cuts off [a portion] of all of them with a sprinkling of ghee,
without making a blunder.
If he does not cut off from one, he makes a blunder;
no blunder befalls him.
The first is made of rice, then one made of barley, then one made of rice.
Rice grains are reddish as it were;
this world is reddish as it were;
that [world] is reddish as it were.
Barley grains are white as it were;
the intermediate space is white as it were.
Through the intermediate space these worlds are continuous;
[this is] for the continuity of these worlds.
He should make the last one of wild rice,
for non-tautology.
Three milch cows are the sacrificial gift, three tārpya-cloths, three pieces of gold.
What they embroider into tārpya-cloths is the form of the yajus formulas;
gold [pieces] are of the sāman chants; milch cows are of the meters.
These, indeed, are the forms of the sacrifice;
through its forms he secures the sacrifice.
A garment belongs to all the deities;
when he gives garments,
he secures all the deities.
Gold is splendor and spiritual luster;
when he gives pieces of gold,
he secures splendor and spiritual luster.
A milch cow is threefold-successful: self, calf, and milk;
when he gives milch cows,
he makes him threefold-successful.
He should recite all the meters;
for this is the complete sacrifice;
for all the meters are recited at a Soma sacrifice.
When the sacrifice was emitted, its afterbirth wrapped around it;
that became the tārpya-cloth.
The sacrifice is the Traidhātavī [offering];
when the sacrificer puts on the tārpya-cloth,
he covers himself with his own womb.
He should also recite an uṣṇih and a kakubh among the kindling verses.
The uṣṇih is the virility of the kakubh, the kakubh of the uṣṇih;
he secures them endowed with virility.
He should not conclude with a jagatī;
this is the end of the meters;
he would reach the end, he would cut off the sacrifice.
With the triṣṭubh, "O Agni, three are your steeds, three your seats," he concludes.
The triṣṭubh is vigor and virility; he does not reach the end;
he establishes him in vigor and virility.

12.6.m:
A horse must not be accepted.
Indeed, this one has teeth in both jaws;
he who accepts [it] chews up his own faculty and virility;
therefore it must not be accepted.
Varuṇa accepted it first;
he went to his own deity;
Varuṇa seized him.
He sacrificed with this oblation to Varuṇa,
for freedom from Varuṇa.
He who accepts a horse should sacrifice with this oblation to Varuṇa,
for freedom from Varuṇa.
It is on four potsherds;
the horse has four feet;
manifestly he releases him from Varuṇa's fetter.
As to that, they say:
"Indeed, he is not released from the others by that which releases him from one."
As many horses as he may accept, so many four-potsherd [cakes] he should pour out.
Here and here is Varuṇa's fetter;
wherever Varuṇa's fetter is, from there he releases him from Varuṇa.
There is one extra;
from that which they do not think of, he releases him thereby;
moreover, he appeases by sacrifice what is yet to come of Varuṇa.
Varuṇa seizes him even more who, having sacrificed with this [offering], accepts another horse.
If he intends to accept another horse, he should perform [an offering] to Sūrya and Varuṇa.
He accepts [it] with the deity Sūrya alongside this world;
he releases him from Varuṇa with that which is for Varuṇa.
There is also a caru for Apāṃ Napāt.
The horse is without a womb, born in the waters;
making him possessed of a womb, he firmly establishes him.
There are twenty-one kindling verses;
twenty-one-fold is the year:
five seasons, twelve months, three are these worlds, that sun is the twenty-first; this is Prajāpati.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati;
he obtains the horse.
"O King Varuṇa, whatever fetter of yours has the gāyatrī meter, that [fetter] of yours I appease by sacrifice with this, svāhā to it!
O King Varuṇa, whatever fetter of yours has the triṣṭubh meter, the jagatī meter, the anuṣṭubh meter, that [fetter] of yours I appease by sacrifice with this, svāhā to it!"
Thus [he says].
So many are the meters;
the meters are Varuṇa's fetters;
with them he seizes him whom Varuṇa seizes.
Here and here is Varuṇa's fetter;
wherever Varuṇa's fetter is, from there he releases him from Varuṇa.
There are two [oblations] for Apāṃ Napāt;
whatever two watery fetters he has, by those two he releases him from them.
He offers these six [oblations];
six are the seasons;
he firmly stands in the seasons.

12.8.m:
For a full year, indeed, his meters are exhausted, whoever sacrifices with soma.
The Devikās are the meters;
he renders the meters unexhausted, renewing their strength.
A four-year-old unbred cow is the sacrificial gift;
for she is hope;
he is anointed toward hope;
he creates hope for him.
One who desires cattle should sacrifice with the Devikās.
The Devikās are the meters;
the meters are a pair: Anumati is the Gāyatrī, Rākā the Triṣṭubh, Sinīvālī the Jagatī, Kuhū the Anuṣṭubh, Dhātṛ the Vaṣaṭkāra.
Because two are lower and two are higher, that is a pair;
because Dhātṛ is the Vaṣaṭkāra, that is a pair.
From that very pair he generates cattle for him.
The full moon day that is former is Anumati; that which is later is Rākā.
The new moon day that is former is Sinīvālī; that which is later is Kuhū. The moon is Dhātṛ.
Because it dwells full in one and diminished in the other, that is a pair.
Because they see the one and not the other, that is a pair.
Because the moon is born from the new moon day, that is a pair.
From that very pair he generates cattle for him.
One who desires offspring should sacrifice with the Devikās.
He should place Dhātṛ last.
The Devikās are females, Dhātṛ is the male;
the offspring, turning away, take the seed;
in them turning away, this seed is placed.
As to that, they say:
"Indeed, he does not place it away because the former [female] is impregnated."
He should place Dhātṛ in between;
he makes all of them rejoicing in the male.
He finds a son, but he becomes one who walks behind [others];
for he leads him around behind with women.
If a [son] should be born, having placed Dhātṛ in front, he should then offer this very [sacrifice];
he leads him around to the very front.
A sick person should sacrifice with the Devikās.
He should place Dhātṛ in the middle.
Dhātṛ is the year;
for the year is confused for him, and so he falls sick;
having arranged the year in the middle for him, he then regenerates him from this pair.
These unappeased meters are capable of wiping him out;
he should seize an animal victim,
for appeasement, for non-wiping-out.
Some animals stand in virility, others do not stand in virility.
Those that are puroḍāśas in front stand in virility;
those that are puroḍāśas behind do not stand in virility.
Those that stand in virility attend while enjoying;
those that do not stand in virility perish.
Those that are puroḍāśas in front stand in virility;
they are released toward Prajāpati as a support.
In the pots in which the somas are placed, in those he should place the Devikās;
for soma is a placer of seed.
One who gives away all his possessions should sacrifice with the Devikās.
Indeed, he who gives all is deprived of cattle.
The Devikās are meters, the meters are cattle;
here he sees the cattle in the lowest place, where he divides them;
from wherever he sees them in the lowest place, from there he regenerates them.
Whoever sacrifices with soma should sacrifice with the Devikās.
The Devikās are the meters;
he renders the meters unexhausted, renewing their strength.

12.10.m:
Viśvarūpa, son of Tvaṣṭṛ, had three heads; he was the nephew of the Asuras.
With one head he drank soma;
with one he consumed food;
with one he drank liquor.
Indra thought:
"Surely this one will become this [all]!"
He grappled with him;
he brought about a joint death with him.
He said to a carpenter standing [by]:
"Run up, cut off his heads!"
The carpenter, having run up, cut off his heads with an axe.
Therefore a carpenter's head is held [liable];
therefore his food is unfit to be eaten.
The head of his that was the soma-drinking one became a kapiñjala bird;
the one that was the food-eating one, a partridge;
the one that was the liquor-drinking one, a sparrow;
therefore it chirps as if continuously burning.
Tvaṣṭṛ, whose son was slain, brought soma without Indra.
He pressed it below in a thousand-holed [vessel];
then they say:
in a hundred-holed [vessel].
Indra, having taken it away from him by force, drank it off through a tube.
He was deprived of the soma-drinking in every direction.
Therefore soma is not to be drunk by one uninvited;
he becomes deprived of the soma-drinking.
What was released from his nostrils, those two became lions;
what [came] from his eyes, those two became tigers;
what [came] from his ears, those two became wolves;
what [came] from below, that was the Sautrāmaṇī.
What he first spat out, that became the jujube [kuvala];
what [he spat out] second, that was the badara [jujube];
what [he spat out] third, that was the karkandhu [jujube].
The two Aśvins healed him;
they made him sacrifice with this Sautrāmaṇī.
Whatever virilities he was then deprived of, those having obtained, they placed in him.
He for whom soma flows over should sacrifice with this;
for he from whom soma flows over is deprived of virility;
whatever virility there is, that he places in him.
This very [rite] also takes place in the royal consecration;
for he who is consecrated by the royal consecration is deprived of virility;
for he slays Vṛtra;
whatever virility there is, that he places in him.
Desiring to flourish, either a brahmin or a nobleman should sacrifice with this very [rite].
Virility is indeed so much;
whatever virility there is, that he places in him.
Regarding that, they say:
"This is a sacrifice for the afflicted; one who is not afflicted should not sacrifice with it."
Regarding that, they say:
"One should sacrifice indeed;
for every person is afflicted;
everyone desires to flourish;
therefore one should sacrifice."
In that there is [a cup] for the Aśvins:
for the two Aśvins healed [him].
In that there is [a cup] for Sarasvatī:
Sarasvatī is indeed speech;
for they healed [him] with speech.
In that there is [a cup] for Indra:
for they placed that virility in Indra.
With kuvala groats he mixes the [cup] for the Aśvins;
with badara groats, that for Indra;
with karkandhu groats, that for Sarasvatī.
Sarasvatī is speech;
he places sweetness in his speech itself.
Moreover, if that speech speaks ill of him, it again speaks well of him.
The Adhvaryu should meditate in his mind on the two lions;
the Pratiprasthātṛ on the two tigers;
the sacrificer on the two wolves.
There is liquor;
whatever virilities he was then deprived of, those having obtained, the two place in him.

12.11.m:
He should set it down on the head of a brahmin;
thereby it is fit for sacrifice.
Liquor is food;
food is fit for sacrifice;
thereby it is fit for sacrifice.
There is one introductory verse, one offering verse;
he places virility in him in a single way.
He offers it as a whole oblation;
he places all virility in him.
He should make a brahmin drink it;
thereby it is made an oblation.
It may be drunk by himself;
he places virility in himself.
That is not to be heeded.
Liquor is evil;
he is seized by evil in the middle when he passes through [the middle];
he releases him from evil from the middle itself.
If he were to offer in the northern fire, he would not turn away from evil; a remedy would be made.
Because he offers in the southern fire,
he turns away from evil; a remedy is made.
Because he causes it to trickle out,
for this [evil] trickles away, as it were.
That which was his deprivation, that which was liable to death, went then to the ancestors.
Because they consecrate with [verses] containing the word 'ancestor',
they stir him up from the ancestors.
Because [they do so] with three each,
for the ancestors are in the third world.
Because there are four [verses],
they stir him up from these four quarters.
It has a hundred syllables;
man has a life of a hundred [years] and a virility of a hundred [powers];
he obtains life and virility.
He buys the shoots from a eunuch with lead;
for a eunuch is untruth, lead is untruth, liquor is untruth;
by means of untruth he buys untruth from untruth.
A mare is the sacrificial gift,
for she is swift;
liquor is swift, as it were;
for success.

12.13.m:
Svarbhānu, the Asura, pierced the sun with darkness.
It did not shine out.
From it the gods took away the darkness.
What they took away first, that became a black sheep;
what [they took away] second, that was a reddish-brown [sheep];
what [they took away] third, that was a white [sheep];
what they took away from the bone, that became a barren sheep.
Now at that time this [earth] was bald and hairless.
The gods seized that [sheep] for Aditi, for desire;
by it they made hair grow on her;
then this [earth] received hair.
He should seize this very [sheep] thus for Aditi, for desire;
whatever desire he desires, that he wins.
A downward ray of that sun stood opposite the Cātvāla trench;
these two united as a pair; she became a barren sheep.
Now at that time these plants were fruitless.
The gods seized her for the Ādityas, for desires;
by her they made [the plants] bear good fruit;
then these [plants] bore fruit.
He should seize both of these thus:
one for Aditi for desire, the other for the Ādityas for desires;
he seizes [them] from both creations for desire.
He should seize a ewe for Sarasvatī, from whom speech departs.
Sarasvatī is speech;
Sarasvatī departs from him from whom speech departs.
That it is for Sarasvatī,
having obtained speech directly, he secures it.
It has not shed its teeth,
for wholeness;
it is unblemished,
for completeness.
Whichever is born the last of triplets, he should seize it for vigor;
vigor is desire.
These two are born to separate teats,
and this one is born to vigor itself;
he secures vigor by obtaining it with this.
The sacrificial post is made of udumbara wood.
Where the gods divided vigor, from there the udumbara tree was born.
That it is made of udumbara wood,
[is] for the securing of vigor.
One desiring offspring or one desiring cattle should seize this very [animal] for Soma and Pūṣan.
Soma is the placer of semen;
Pūṣan is the begetter;
Soma places semen in him; Pūṣan begets [it];
he finds offspring or cattle, for whichever desire he seizes [it].
Desiring to thrive, he should seize a white he-goat for Vāyu.
Vāyu is the swiftest of the gods;
he runs up to him with his own share;
he leads him most quickly to prosperity;
he wipes off;
he runs forward;
he leads him to prosperity.
This one [who thrives] becomes active, both meritorious and as it were so-so.
Desiring relatives, he should seize for Vāyu Niyutvat a white he-goat with spotted ears.
These creatures, bound by the nose, follow Vāyu hither and thither.
Vāyu is the breath;
Vāyu is the clan of the gods;
the Niyuts are the guide, the clan of the gods;
he runs up to him with his own share;
he brings the clan to him.
That it is for [Vāyu] Niyutvat,
he makes a support,
for non-wiping-out.
For desires, he should seize a white he-goat for Vāyu.
He who desires, desires subtly;
Vāyu blows subtly along the gods;
he runs up to him with his own share;
he blows along his desires.

13.3.m:
One who has a rival should seize a dwarf [animal] dedicated to Viṣṇu.
Viṣṇu indeed won these worlds;
he drove out the Asuras from these worlds.
He indeed wins these worlds;
he drives out the rival from these worlds.
He should sacrifice on uneven ground,
for uneven, as it were, are these worlds.
He should seize [an animal] with speckled thighs dedicated to Indra and the Maruts, from which kṣatriya the clan would withdraw aside.
Indra indeed is the nobility; the Maruts are the clan.
He attaches the clan right to the nobility.
It is one with speckled thighs;
he puts the clan right behind him,
for non-mingling.
One who desires cattle should seize [an animal] with a raised spine dedicated to Indra.
Indra indeed uncovered Vala;
a thousand followed out after him.
Before it, this [animal] pierced upwards;
seeing these worlds, wishing to pierce upwards, it shrank together.
Therefore it is compressed, with its neck bent back.
He should seize this one before the thousand;
he obtains a thousand cattle;
he indeed uncovers Vala.
When he should obtain a thousand cattle, then he should seize a dwarf [animal] dedicated to Viṣṇu,
for firm standing.
Upon this, indeed, that thousand stood;
being stood upon, it branched apart.
Therefore it is stretched out sideways, as it were.
He should seize these thus on both sides,
for seizing the thousand.
Sacrifice, indeed, desired Dakṣiṇā;
he united with her.
She conceived an embryo;
casting it aside, she ran away.
Aditi noticed it lying cast aside;
she brought it [to herself];
she placed it under her thighs;
it grew under her thighs.
Therefore it is stretched sideways, as it were,
for it grew under the thighs.
Viṣṇu noticed it;
he laid claim to it.
Concerning it they went for an inquiry; they decided it for Viṣṇu.
Therefore they say: 'The son belongs to the maker alone.'
By whichever dwarf he would wish to prosper, for that one he should beforehand offer a caru to Aditi.
Aditi indeed made this one grow;
he ransoms it from her.
With that [animal] free from debt, ransomed, fit for sacrifice, and released, he indeed prospers.
One who would hope for kingship should seize a brown bull, with a prominent chest, dedicated to Soma.
Soma indeed is king of the gods;
Soma is the deity of him who hopes for kingship.
He runs to his own deity with his share;
he leads him to kingship.
In that it has a prominent chest,
that is the form of virility.
He should seize a red bull, with a prominent chest, dedicated to Indra the thunderbolt-wielder, for a nobleman who is either practicing sorcery or wishing to thrive.
Noble indeed is the nobleman by deity;
the thunderbolt is his virility.
He places the thunderbolt in him;
by it he conquers;
he indeed thrives.
In that it has a prominent chest,
that is the form of virility.
Whichever is born on the Ekāṣṭakā, he should let that one loose.
If two should be born, he should let both loose.
If a second should be born in a year, he should let that one loose after a year.
He should offer an eight-potsherd [cake] to Agni when they are born;
cattle indeed belong to Agni;
from him he ransoms them.
He should offer a twelve-potsherd [cake] to Agni Vaiśvānara when the year has come round;
the year indeed is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he ransoms them from the year until the time they become fit for sacrifice.
Year after year he should offer a twelve-potsherd [cake] to Agni Vaiśvānara;
the year indeed is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he ransoms them year after year.
With both of them ransomed on all sides, fit for sacrifice, and released, he indeed prospers.
On this [day], indeed, Indra was born;
he is the most vigorous of the gods.
Therefore, whichever of cattle is born on the Ekāṣṭakā, that becomes vigorous.
Vigorous becomes he who, knowing thus, sacrifices with these two.
He should seize the former for Indra the destroyer of rivals;
a rival is evil;
he indeed wards off evil.
The latter for Indra who overcomes Vṛtra;
a rival is evil;
having warded off evil, he then becomes an overcomer of Vṛtra by this;
he enters upon self-rule.
No other among his equals becomes wealthier than he who, knowing thus, sacrifices with these two.
A hundred is the sacrificial fee of one; a horse drawing the yoke in front is that of the other.
Whatever two Brāhmaṇas are at hand in this [sacrifice],
he should seize [the animals] without letting them loose in this manner;
by both he indeed prospers.
Whatever he gives there, that is the sacrificial fee.

13.5.m:
One whose father and grandfather were virtuous but who does not attain that should seize a he-goat dedicated to Agni [and] a bull dedicated to Soma.
Agni is all the deities;
he seizes the deities right from the mouth.
Verily, he is deprived of Indra's power, of the Soma-drinking, whose father and grandfather are virtuous but who does not attain that.
A brāhmaṇa belongs to Soma by deity;
Indra's power is his Soma-drinking, in that [the animal] is dedicated to Soma;
he causes him to prosper with Indra's power, with the Soma-drinking.
One who is excluded, or who has been excluded for a long time, should seize a milch cow with calves of various ages dedicated to Heaven and Earth.
Verily, he is excluded from Heaven and Earth who is excluded, or excluded for a long time.
In that it is dedicated to Heaven and Earth,
he gives him a share in Heaven and Earth.
Verily, he is like one [belonging to] various ages who is excluded, or excluded for a long time;
various-aged is his deity.
In that it is [a cow] with calves of various ages,
his own beast through that is of this [deity] alone.
He should seize a calf dedicated to Vāyu on the morrow.
Vāyu, verily, is the calf of these two;
he swells these two;
he causes [them] to yield.
He runs to him with his own share;
he causes these two to yield for him.
One whose father and grandfather did not drink Soma, but who himself desires to drink, should seize this dappled [beast] produced along with [another] dedicated to Indra and Agni.
Verily, he is deprived of Indra's power, of the Soma-drinking, whose father and grandfather do not drink Soma.
A brāhmaṇa belongs to Agni by deity;
Indra's power is his Soma-drinking, in that [the offering] is dedicated to Indra and Agni;
by his own deity he continues the Soma-drinking.
Verily, the Soma-drinking of him whose father and grandfather do not drink Soma is produced along with [another];
in that it is produced along with [another],
his own beast through that is of him;
in that it is dappled,
that is the form of [an offering] belonging to two deities.
One who desires, 'May a well-proportioned son be born to me,' should seize a gelded [beast] dedicated to Tvaṣṭṛ.
Tvaṣṭṛ, verily, is the fashioner of forms;
he runs to him with his own share;
he fashions forms for him.
One who has a rival should seize [a beast] with a black blaze dedicated to the Aśvins.
The gods, verily, drove the Asuras away from the day;
what was their own, what was their property, what was to be known, with that they entered into the night.
The gods saw this [beast] with a black blaze dedicated to the Aśvins;
they seized it;
by it they followed them into the night.
The Aśvins, verily, are Heaven and Earth;
having hemmed them in from below with Heaven and Earth, and from beyond with the sun, they drove them from the night.
What was their own, what was their property, what was to be known, that they wrested away.
Then the gods prospered, the Asuras were defeated.
The Aśvins, verily, are Heaven and Earth;
having hemmed in the rival from below with Heaven and Earth, and from beyond with the sun, he drives [him] from day and night;
what is his own, what is his property, what is to be known, that he wrests away;
he prospers in himself, his rival is defeated.
In that it has a blaze,
that is the form of the day;
in that it is black,
that is of the night;
he drives him out from both colours.
Indra, verily, desired Vilisteṅgā, the Dānavī;
he lived among the Asuras;
he became a woman among women, a man among men;
he thought himself to be seized by Nirṛti, as it were.
He saw this unmanly [beast] dedicated to Indra and Nirṛti;
in that form in which he lived, that he seized.
What of it was not to be cut off into portions, with that he proceeded first:
'Going away to the south, on a natural cleft, may Nirṛti partake, being pleased! Svāhā!'
Then, returning, he concluded the other [part] dedicated to Indra.
Having cut off a portion for Nirṛti first, he then placed Indra's power and manly vigour in himself through [the part] dedicated to Indra.
Then, verily, he prospered.
One who desires to prosper, who thinks himself to be seized by Nirṛti as it were, should seize an unmanly [beast] dedicated to Indra and Nirṛti.
What of it would not be to be cut off into portions, with that he should proceed first:
'Going away to the south, on a natural cleft, may Nirṛti partake, being pleased! Svāhā!'
Then, returning, he should conclude the other [part] dedicated to Indra.
Having cut off a portion for Nirṛti first, he places Indra's power and manly vigour in himself through [the part] dedicated to Indra;
he indeed prospers.
In battle, one who desires, 'May this army not conquer,' should seize a wide-horned bull dedicated to Indra.
Indra, verily, is the leader of battle;
Indra is the bringer of victory;
he runs to him with his own share;
he leads these away.
In that it is wide-horned,
that is the form of dispersing.
At the junction of two tribes, one who desires, 'May I fall upon both tribes,' should seize a wide-horned bull for Indra the Destroyer.
He falls upon both tribes;
they go about slaying one another.
In that it is wide-horned,
he causes the two to go to complete destruction.

13.7.m:
He should seize three bulls with a blaze in spring, three with white humps in summer, three with white posteriors in autumn.
In that they have a blaze,
thereby he places fiery splendour right at the front.
In that they have white humps,
thereby he places lustre right in the middle.
In that they have white posteriors,
thereby he places spiritual lustre right at the top.
They are seized all around the year.
The year, verily, is manly vigour;
he obtains the manly vigour that is the year itself.
They are nine.
Nine are the breaths;
he places the breaths in himself.
They should belong to Indra; one desiring to prosper should sacrifice [with them].
Yonder sun, verily, is Indra;
he prospered by means of these;
he placed fiery splendour and manly vigour in himself by means of these.
He indeed prospers, he places fiery splendour and manly vigour in himself, who knowing thus sacrifices with these.
He should seize a tenth one belonging to Prajāpati, of all forms, when the year has come round,
for the attainment of the year.
Prajāpati is the womb;
he is established in the womb itself.
It is of all forms
for the attainment of all,
for the acquisition of all.
They are ten.
Ten are the breaths;
the breaths are manly vigour;
he is established in manly vigour itself.
One desirous of food should seize two milch-cows belonging to Heaven and Earth, having a common mother.
Heaven and Earth, verily, rule over food.
From yonder it rains, in this one [earth] it is established.
These two are the bestowers of food;
he approaches these two with their share;
these two bestow food upon him.
They are having a common mother;
for these two are, as it were, having a common mother.
On the next day he should seize for these two a calf dedicated to Vāyu.
Vāyu, verily, is the calf of these two;
he causes these two to swell;
he causes [them] to give [milk].
He approaches him with his share;
he causes these two to give [milk] to him.
One desirous of spiritual lustre should seize [an animal] with a white back dedicated to Bṛhaspati.
Bṛhaspati, verily, is sacred power;
Bṛhaspati is the bestower of spiritual lustre.
He approaches him with his share;
he bestows spiritual lustre upon him.
A back, as it were, he becomes, who prospers.
In that it has a white back,
he makes him a back itself.
A younger brother who desires precedence should seize [an animal] black with a blaze dedicated to the Aśvins.
The Aśvins, verily, were younger brothers among the gods;
they reached the forefront.
The Aśvins are the deity of this one who is a younger brother;
he takes hold from behind of them;
they lead him around to the forefront.
He who is a younger brother is seized by evil;
evil is black, as it were.
In that it is black,
he strikes away evil itself.
In that it has a blaze,
thereby he places fiery splendour right at the front.
One who is sick should seize [an animal] black with a blaze dedicated to the Aśvins.
The Aśvins, verily, are the physicians of the gods;
the Aśvins are the deity of this one who is sick;
he approaches them with their share;
they heal him.
He who is sick is seized by evil;
evil is black, as it were.
In that it is black,
he strikes away evil itself.
In that it has a blaze,
thereby he places fiery splendour right at the front.
One desirous of offspring or desirous of cattle should seize an unmanly [animal] dedicated to Tvaṣṭṛ.
Prajāpati, verily, desiring to emit offspring,
did not find a second pair.
Having made this form, he united with himself by means of his thumb;
thence he emitted offspring.
Tvaṣṭṛ is the producer of the pair;
he approaches him with his share;
he produces cattle for him from this pair.
By this form, verily, Prajāpati emitted offspring;
by the very form by which Prajāpati emitted offspring, from that very form he is propagated with offspring and cattle.
He who is afraid of impotence should seize an unmanly [animal] dedicated to Indra.
Indra, verily, struck away this skin, this evil;
he approaches him with his share;
he strikes away from him that skin, that evil.
A bull is the sacrificial fee;
a bull, verily, is manly vigour;
he places manly vigour in him.

13.8.m:
The metres, verily, envied the sacrifice.
The Vaṣaṭ-call, having approached, cut off the head of the Gāyatrī.
Then the sap that flowed out became a barren cow.
Because it flowed according to will (vaśam), that is the nature of the barren cow (vaśā).
The blood, verily, which flowed first,
that Bṛhaspati received;
she became the reddish one;
therefore she belongs to Bṛhaspati.
That which flowed second, Mitra and Varuṇa received;
she became the two-formed one;
therefore she belongs to Mitra and Varuṇa.
That which flowed third, the All-gods received;
she became the multiform one;
therefore she belongs to the All-gods.
That which sprinkled upward, Bṛhaspati received;
he became an ox (ukṣan);
that is the nature of an ox (ukṣatva).
That which escaped beyond, those are these other forms.
One desirous of spiritual lustre should seize a reddish [cow] dedicated to Bṛhaspati.
He is deprived of sap, verily, who, being fit for spiritual lustre, does not possess spiritual lustre.
This sap of the metres is sacred power, [is] Bṛhaspati;
by sacred power itself he places fiery splendour and sap in him;
he becomes possessed of spiritual lustre.
One desirous of rain should seize a two-formed [cow] dedicated to Mitra and Varuṇa.
Mitra and Varuṇa, verily, are day and night;
Parjanya rains following day and night;
for he rains by night or by day.
These two rule over rain;
he approaches them with their share;
they, pleased, bring down rain for him.
One desirous of offspring should seize a two-formed [cow] dedicated to Mitra and Varuṇa.
Mitra and Varuṇa, verily, are day and night; offspring are born following day and night;
for they are born by night or by day.
He propagates them following day and night.
He should seize a multiform [cow] dedicated to the All-gods for desires.
The All-gods, verily, are desires;
he approaches them with their share;
they grant him all desires.
One desiring to prosper should seize an ox dedicated to Bṛhaspati.
He is deprived of sap, verily, who, being fit for prosperity, does not prosper.
This sap of the metres is sacred power, [is] Bṛhaspati;
by sacred power itself he places prosperity and sap in him;
he indeed prospers.
One desirous of lustre should seize a white [cow] dedicated to Sūrya.
Yonder sun, verily, is the bestower of lustre;
he approaches him with his share;
he bestows lustre upon him.
Practising sorcery, he should seize a brown [cow] dedicated to Brahmaṇaspati.
Brahmaṇaspati, verily, is sacred power;
by sacred power itself he directs [sorcery] against him.
This, verily, is the form of sacred power: that it is brown;
by the form itself he whets it.

14.3.m:
You are taken with a support.
Sitting on wood, sitting among men, sitting in life, I take you agreeable to Indra.
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
You are taken with a support.
Sitting in waters, sitting in ghee, sitting in beings, I take you agreeable to Indra.
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
You are taken with a support.
Sitting on earth, sitting in the intermediate space, sitting in the firmament, I take you agreeable to Indra.
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
You are taken with a support;
O cup, beneficial to all men, within the sage he has sat down.
The three supreme ones which were born, the divine vessel was pressed down;
of them, the uncoiled ones, I have grasped the sap, the nourishment.
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
You are taken with a support.
I have raised up the sap of the waters, the bright [fluid] gathered together in the sun;
what is the sap of the sap of the waters, that highest of yours I take.
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
Creating deeds by this station, for he, glowing, wide, is a path to the choice [reward];
he has risen up against the foundation, the tip of the mead, when his own self sent forth the body.
You are taken with a support;
for Prajāpati, you!
This is your womb;
for Prajāpati, you!
Surely, as those possessing barley reap the barley in due order, having separated it,
make here and there the food of those who have not gone to the reverence-paying of the sacrificial grass!
You are taken with a support;
for Prajāpati, you!
This is your womb;
for Prajāpati, you!

14.5.m:
The gods indeed saw the sacrifices separately: 'This one for me, this one for you.'
Then they all saw this [sacrifice].
They strove for it;
they ran a race for it;
Bṛhaspati won it.
Bṛhaspati, indeed, is the domestic priest of the gods.
Indeed, what priestly power the domestic priest hears, that belongs to the king;
with it he made Indra sacrifice.
He attained self-rule.
That is this self-ruling sacrifice;
he who sacrifices with this attains self-rule.
Because Bṛhaspati won it,
therefore a brāhmaṇa should sacrifice [with it];
because he made Indra sacrifice,
therefore a nobleman [should sacrifice with it].
The gods, indeed, brought forth the sacrifices separately;
then Prajāpati brought forth this one.
In it the gods desired a share;
to them he gave the meters as their victories.
Then Prajāpati sacrificed with this;
he attained self-rule.
That is this self-ruling sacrifice;
he who sacrifices with this attains self-rule.
Food, indeed, is vigor;
he who knowing thus eats food, indeed wins vigor.
Soma is the Vājapeya;
he who knowing thus drinks Soma, goes indeed to vigor.
For as many of the gods as drank Soma, they went to vigor;
therefore everyone desires to drink Soma.
Speech, indeed, is the impulse of vigor.
That speech, when created, divided fourfold.
Three quarters are in these worlds, one quarter among the beasts.
That which is in heaven, is in the Bṛhat [sāman], it is in the thunder;
that which is in the intermediate space, is in the wind, it is in the Vāmadevya [sāman];
that which is on earth, is in fire, it is in the Rathantara [sāman];
that which is among the beasts, what remained over of it, that they placed in the brāhmaṇa;
therefore the brāhmaṇa speaks both kinds of speech: divine and human.
He performs heroic deed by speech who knows thus.
By that which is in the Bṛhat and Rathantara [sāmans], the sacrifice comes to him;
by that which is among the beasts, [he attains] the sacrifice without ritual error.
Indeed, it comes to him from an unexpected quarter, who knows thus;
for he knows as much as speech is.
For by speech one gives;
by speech it is given away.
He who gains [wealth] through verses and praise-songs, from him one should not accept [gifts];
for through untruth he gains [it];
for verse is untruth, praise-song is untruth.
From an intoxicated person one should not accept [gifts];
for an intoxicated person is untruth;
for when he becomes sober, then that hurts him.

14.6.m:
These seventeen dual cups belong to Prajāpati.
Seventeenfold, indeed, is man, belonging to Prajāpati: four limbs, head and neck, trunk, speech as the seventh, [and] ten breaths.
Upon limb after limb, indeed, evil clings to man.
In that he draws the cups alternately,
he releases him from evil from limb after limb.
Splendor, indeed, is Soma; evil is the wine cups.
In that, when the time has come, they run forward to the east with the Soma [cups], [and] backward to the west with the wine cups,
he cleanses him thoroughly from evil.
Therefore they say: 'The Vājapeya sacrificer is purified indeed,'
for he cleanses him thoroughly from evil.
These five cups are drawn;
fivefold is the sacrifice;
he wins the sacrifice itself.
They all belong to Indra;
all at once he bestows Indra-power upon him.
This is the highest food of the gods, namely Soma;
this is the lowest nourishment of men, namely wine.
In that both the Soma cups and the wine cups are drawn together,
by the highest food he wins the lower nourishment.
This is the luster of priestly power, namely Soma;
wine is the impurity of food.
In that both the Soma cups and the wine cups are drawn together,
by the luster of priestly power he places luster in himself;
by the impurity of food he strikes away impurity.
He wins the world of the gods by the Soma cups, the world of men by the wine cups.
He saves himself by the Soma cups, his wife by the wine cups.
Therefore they say: 'The Vājapeya sacrificer comes into being in yonder world,'
for by the sacrifice it has been won by him.
With the Sāvitrī verse, 'O god Savitṛ, urge forth the sacrifice, urge forth the lord of sacrifice unto good fortune,' he offers at each joint.
This is an omitted sacrifice;
who indeed knows how much of it they perform, how much not?
[It is] for wholeness, for impulse.
With 'In the impulse of vigor the great mother,' he brings down the chariot;
this is Aditi;
impelled both by her and by Savitṛ, he brings [it] down
for the winning of vigor.
With 'Within the waters is immortality, within the waters is healing,' he washes the horses;
the horses are born of the waters;
from their own womb he brings them forth;
and he makes them fit for offering, worthy of sacrifice.
With 'May Vāyu [yoke] thee, or may Manu [yoke] thee,' he yokes them;
a human is not worthy to yoke them;
with the deities he yokes them.
With 'O Son of Waters, of swift impulse,' he wipes their foreheads;
he repeats what has already been said before.

14.9.m:
Now these additional cups [are drawn],
for the securing of whatever supreme food is not yet secured.
Now these animal victims [are offered],
for the securing of the sacrificial rites.
In that there is one sacred to Agni,
he thereby attains the Agniṣṭoma;
for the Agniṣṭoma is established in Agni.
In that there is one sacred to Indra and Agni,
he thereby attains the Ukthas;
for the Ukthas belong to Indra and Agni.
In that there is one sacred to Indra,
he thereby attains the Ṣoḍaśin;
for the Ṣoḍaśin belongs to Indra.
He wins the self by the Agniṣṭoma, out-breath and in-breath by the Uktha, vigor by the chant of the Ṣoḍaśin, speech by the Atirātra, offspring by the chant of the Bṛhat.
In this world he establishes himself by the Agniṣṭoma, in the intermediate space by the Uktha, in the heavenly world by the chant of the Ṣoḍaśin, in the paths leading to the gods by the Atirātra, in the firmament by the chant of the Bṛhat.
So many, indeed, are the divine worlds;
as many divine worlds as there are, them he attains.
Now this ewe sacred to Sarasvatī [is offered],
for the securing of yonder seventeenth chant which is not yet secured.
Now this dappled barren cow sacred to the Maruts [is offered].
The gods and the asuras were in this world;
half were the gods, half were the asuras.
Those gods saw this dappled barren cow sacred to the Maruts.
They seized it;
by it they tore away the second world, the world of the asuras.
Of whomsoever they cut off [the sacrificial portion], his is the divine world; of whom they do not cut off, his is the asura world.
By this [victim] he tears away the second world, the world of the rival;
for the rival is in the second world.
The victim sacred to Sarasvatī is the last of these five;
the victim sacred to Sarasvat is the last of the other seventeen.
Sarasvatī, indeed, is speech;
with speech he extends the sacrifice.
What of the sacrifice a knowing one does not perform, or what an unknowing one omits, that is a flaw;
that he arranges with speech, with Sarasvatī.
These seventeen animal victims sacred to Prajāpati are seized.
Seventeenfold is Prajāpati;
he obtains Prajāpati.
They are of one color;
he places vigor in him in a single manner.
The victim sacred to Sarasvat is the last;
Sarasvatī, indeed, is speech;
he establishes himself in speech.

14.10.m:
The sāman is the Rathantara,
for the winning of blessings.
This [earth], indeed, is the Rathantara;
upon it he is anointed.
The Vājapeya, indeed, is out of womb [in origin], being enunciated, belonging to Prajāpati,
having an enunciated sāman;
thereby it is out of womb.
In that the morning pressings are unenunciated,
thereby it has a womb.
The morning pressings are unenunciated;
inenunciated is Prajāpati;
he obtains Prajāpati.
They praise with verses containing the word 'prize' (vājavatī) at midday.
Food, indeed, is the prize,
for the securing of food.
[They praise] with verses containing the word 'bright' (citravatī) at the Ārbhava-pavamāna,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
The Āṣṭrādam̐ṣṭra is the last of the Ukthas;
he connects it with the subsequent two chants.
The sāman is the Bṛhat.
What is redundant, that is redundant towards yonder world;
the Bṛhat, indeed, is capable of attaining yonder world.
The Bṛhat is virility, vigor;
he firmly establishes himself in virility, in vigor.
They praise on verses to Viṣṇu containing [the name] Śipiviṣṭa.
This, indeed, is Prajāpati's form that abides in cattle: that which contains [the name] Śipiviṣṭa.
Therefore they praise on verses containing [the name] Śipiviṣṭa.
Redundant, indeed, is this; redundant is Śipiviṣṭa;
by means of the redundant he obtains the redundant.
// Thus the fourteenth chapter, named Vājapeya, in the Iṭhimikā of the Caraka recension of the glorious Yajus, in the Kāṭhaka, is complete.

15.5.m:
There is a vessel made from a naturally fallen branch of an aśvattha tree.
They milk a white [cow] having a white calf.
It solidifies by itself, is churned by itself, dissolves by itself.
Then he pours out the oblation sacred to Mitra and Bṛhaspati.
He divides those rice grains into two parts.
Those that are smaller, they cook into that caru sacred to Bṛhaspati.
When that is cooked, then, having covered that aśvattha vessel, he pours in clarified butter using a strainer.
There he pours the larger ones.
That caru sacred to Mitra becomes cooked by itself.
They make half the altar;
half is naturally made. They mow half the sacrificial grass;
half is naturally cut. They make half the fuel;
half is naturally made.
A white-backed [bull] is the sacrificial gift for the [offering] sacred to Bṛhaspati;
a horse for that sacred to Mitra,
or that white [cow] having a white calf.
For Agni the lord of the house (gṛhapati), an [offering] of fast-growing rice on eight potsherds;
for Savitṛ the impeller, an [offering] of satīna-peas on eight potsherds;
for Soma the lord of the forest, a caru of śyāmāka-grain;
for Bṛhaspati the lord of speech, a caru of wild rice (nīvāra);
for Indra the eldest, an [offering] of year-old rice on eleven potsherds;
for Mitra the lord of truth, a caru of āmbā-grain;
for Varuṇa the lord of order, an [offering] of barley on ten potsherds;
for Rudra the lord of cattle, a caru of gavidhukā-seeds.
'May Savitṛ impel you of impellings;
Agni of household matters;
Soma of the trees of the forest;
Bṛhaspati of speeches;
Indra of seniorities;
Mitra of truths;
Varuṇa of orders;
Rudra of cattle.
O gods, impel this one, free from rivals, to great dominion, to great seniority, to great kingship, to great rule over the people, to great overlordship of the whole universe!'
